<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Neo-Assyrian Empire</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Neo-Assyrian</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Neo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire</a> was the final and greatest phase of Assyrian power, beginning with a revival after 911 BCE and ending with the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nineveh">Nineveh</a> in 612 BCE. It built a tribute-extraction system based on conquest, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">mass deportation</a> and provincial administration, and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esarhaddon">Esarhaddon</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 671 BCE. Its wars against the Kushite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaohs</a> drove the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushite-empire">Kushite Empire</a> out of Egypt.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/neo-assyrian-empire.jpg" alt="Neo-Assyrian Empire" width="320" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ningyou · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_Assyria.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Assyrian economy underwent fundamental transformations across a history of some fourteen centuries, from the commercial network of the Old Assyrian period to the tribute-extraction system of the <b>Neo-Assyrian Empire</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Kassite period the sources fall almost silent for several centuries, and Dilmun re-emerges in the eighth century BCE as a minor kingdom paying tribute to the <b>Neo-Assyrian Empire</b>, which valued its position but did not occupy it.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dilmun">Dilmun · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Israel’s last decades were shaped by the expansion of the <b>Neo-Assyrian Empire</b> under Tiglath-pileser III (r. 745–727 BCE).</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-israel">Kingdom of Israel · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 8th century BCE the kingdom at Gordium expanded into an empire dominating most of central and western Anatolia, bordering the larger and more powerful <b>Neo-Assyrian Empire</b> to the south-east and Urartu to the north-east.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-phrygia">Kingdom of Phrygia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Urartu was the most serious and lasting rival the <b>Neo-Assyrian Empire</b> faced in the north, and Assyrian texts, including Sargon II’s long letter to the god Ashur describing the campaign of 714 BCE, are a major source for its history.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-urartu">Kingdom of Urartu · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In the seventh century BCE, however, the empire faced the <b>Neo-Assyrian Empire</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kushite-empire">Kushite Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
